From f10421dda5139bbf9de8f417eb0e5c0b90456d08 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Sun, 30 Aug 2026 03:28:56 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 10353/11362] Correct the exp6753 "stall": it was healthy the
 whole time, at 38 tok/s

I filed a measured-looking known-issues entry (4c2fc36bc2) reporting exp6753 as
stalled in poll, and reported the same thing in four consecutive hourly status
checks. It is wrong. Before killing the process I checked, and found:

  llama-server 3306606   99.4% CPU  state R  17,884 MiB on GPU 0
  child python 3301909    5.0% CPU  blocked in poll -> socket to the server
  parent       3301833    0.3% CPU  blocked in poll -> waiting on the child

  325.24.235.827 I slot print_timing: task 718922 | n_decoded = 1714 | tg = 38.31 t/s

decoding at 38.3 tokens/second, server log written that same minute.

Three compounding errors, all mine:

1. MEASURED THE WRONG PROCESS. "35.21 CPU-seconds, unchanged for three hours" is
   the supervising PARENT, whose job is to wait. The work is in a grandchild. In a
   three-tier harness the parent's CPU time is not a progress signal, and
   wchan=poll on a supervisor is the normal state.

2. READ THE WRONG LOG. The "log-silent while alive" claim came from the nohup
   runner log; the run writes to /tmp/carnot_llama_server_logs/llama_server_*.log,
   which was being appended continuously. It was discoverable from
   /proc/<pid>/fd in the first hour.

3. TREATED AN INDEPENDENT FAILURE AS CONFIRMATION. The conductor's v2 task really
   did hit its wall-clock cap twice, which felt like a second harness reproducing
   the stall. A codex task has an ~80-minute cap and this generation runs for
   hours -- the cap says the work is LONG, not stuck. Two observations agreeing is
   not evidence when both are consistent with the benign explanation.

The original entry is preserved and the correction appended, per never-prune,
because how it failed is more useful than what it concluded. The durable lesson:
liveness, CPU time, GPU utilisation and log mtime are four different questions and
each must be asked of the RIGHT process -- find the worker via
`nvidia-smi --query-compute-apps` and walk ppid, read ITS log via /proc/<pid>/fd,
check ITS io counters.

This came within one command of killing a healthy five-hour run on my own
recommendation.

